## Configuration and environment

- One `.env` in the project root; `.env.example` lists every key with a comment and is committed. Never commit `.env`. Values with spaces or quotes are single-quoted (`APP_NAME='My "Shop"'`); `scripts/env.ts` reads the file with Node's built-in dotenv parser (`parseEnv` from `node:util`) — never parse it by hand. The desktop app does **not** read `.env` at run time: values are applied when the app is built or started with `npm run dev`, so restart `npm run dev` after changing `.env`.
- The app name comes **only** from `APP_NAME` in `.env`. `scripts/tauri.ts` passes it to Tauri as `productName` (window title, macOS menu bar, Dock, `.app`/`.dmg` name; characters a file name cannot contain become spaces), `vite.config.ts` gives it to the UI (`appName` from `src/config.ts`, `%APP_NAME%` in `index.html`), Rust reads it as `app.package_info().name`. Never hardcode the name; `tauri.conf.json` keeps neutral placeholders (`app`, `com.example.app`) on purpose.
- `APP_SLUG` ({{ env.APP_SLUG }}) is the Rust package and binary name in `src-tauri/Cargo.toml`. Dev builds are not bundled, so macOS keeps their WebView data (local storage, caches) in `~/Library/WebKit/{{ env.APP_SLUG }}` and `~/Library/Caches/{{ env.APP_SLUG }}`. To rename it, change `APP_SLUG` and `package.name` together and run `cargo build --manifest-path src-tauri/Cargo.toml` so `Cargo.lock` records the new name.
- `APP_IDENTIFIER` ({{ env.APP_IDENTIFIER }}) is the bundle id and names the release data folder. Replace `com.example` with your reverse domain **before the first release**: changing it later moves the app's data to a new, empty folder.
- `WEB_PORT` ({{ ports.WEB_PORT }}) is the Vite dev server the dev window loads (Tauri's `devUrl`).
{%- if choices.docker != "none" %}
- `COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME` ({{ project.slug }}) names the Docker Compose stack.
{%- endif %}
{%- if choices.docker == "full" %}
- `WEB_TOOLCHAIN=docker` makes `npm run dev`/`build` serve and build the UI in the `dev` container (`host` would use a local `node_modules`).
{%- endif %}
- Only keys listed in `PUBLIC_ENV_KEYS` (`vite.config.ts`) reach the UI, as `import.meta.env.<KEY>`; declare their types in `src/env.d.ts`. Everything in the UI bundle ships inside the app and can be read: never expose secrets.
- To add a setting: add it to `.env.example` and `.env` with a comment; for the UI add it to `PUBLIC_ENV_KEYS` and `src/env.d.ts`; for Tauri configuration extend `tauriConfigPatch()` in `scripts/tauri-config.ts` (with a test); Rust code that needs a build-time value gets it through configuration or a command argument. Runtime settings chosen by the user belong in the database (or a settings file in the data folder), not in `.env`.
- The version is `version` in `src-tauri/Cargo.toml` (Tauri reads it from there); keep `package.json` `version` equal.

## How to

### Add a feature (example: `tasks`)

1. **Migration** `src-tauri/src/db/migrations/002_create_tasks.sql` and append it to `MIGRATIONS` in `src-tauri/src/db/mod.rs` (see below).
2. **Rust module** `src-tauri/src/tasks/`:
   - `model.rs`: `Task` (`#[derive(Serialize)]`, `#[serde(rename_all = "camelCase")]`) and input structs (`#[derive(Deserialize)]`, `deny_unknown_fields`).
   - `repository.rs`: `TasksRepository { db: Arc<Database> }`, SQL only, row mapping, tests on `Database::in_memory()`.
   - `service.rs`: `TasksService`, rules and validation (`AppError::Validation`, `AppError::NotFound`), tests.
   - `commands.rs`: `#[tauri::command] pub async fn list_tasks(tasks: State<'_, TasksService>) -> AppResult<Vec<Task>>`, …
   - `mod.rs`: `pub mod commands; mod model; mod repository; mod service;` and the `pub use` lines.
3. **Wire** it in `src-tauri/src/lib.rs`: `mod tasks;`, `app.manage(tasks::TasksService::new(tasks::TasksRepository::new(db.clone())))` in `setup`, and every command in `generate_handler![...]`.
4. **UI:** `src/api/tasks.ts` (types mirroring `model.rs` + `tasksApi` calling `call('list_tasks')` …) → `src/stores/tasks.ts` → page, route and components → tests in `test/`.

The Notes example is the reference for every layer: `src-tauri/src/notes/`, `src-tauri/src/db/migrations/001_create_notes.sql`, `src/api/notes.ts`, `src/stores/notes.ts`, `src/pages/NotesPage.vue`, `src/components/Note*.vue` and their tests. When it is no longer useful, remove all of these, its route and sidebar link, its commands in `lib.rs`, and add a migration that drops the `notes` table.

### Add a command to an existing feature

Model/input structs → repository method (if SQL is needed) → service method with its rules → `#[tauri::command]` in `commands.rs` → register it in `generate_handler!` → Rust tests → client function in `src/api/<feature>.ts` → store action → UI test. Arguments are passed by name from TypeScript in camelCase (`call('delete_note', { id })`; a Rust argument `note_id` is `noteId` in TypeScript).

### Add a migration

- Create `src-tauri/src/db/migrations/NNN_snake_case.sql` with the next number and append `include_str!("migrations/NNN_snake_case.sql")` to `MIGRATIONS` in `src-tauri/src/db/mod.rs`. Each migration runs once, in its own transaction; `PRAGMA user_version` stores how many have run.
- Never edit, reorder or delete a migration that may have run anywhere (including an installed release); write a new one.
- Use `STRICT` tables, `INTEGER PRIMARY KEY` ids, `snake_case` columns, ISO 8601 UTC timestamps as `TEXT` (default `strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%fZ', 'now')`), and `REFERENCES … ON DELETE …` for relations (foreign keys are on).
- Migrations run when the app starts and in every test database (`Database::in_memory()`). To reset development data, stop the app and delete `data/app.sqlite*`.

### Add a page

1. Create `src/pages/<Name>Page.vue` with `<script setup lang="ts">`.
2. Register it in `src/router/index.ts` with a `name` and a lazy `component: () => import('@/pages/<Name>Page.vue')`.
3. Add it to `links` in `src/components/AppSidebar.vue` (label and a 20×20 outline icon path).
4. Get data from a store; move reusable UI into `src/components/`.

### Use a Tauri plugin or a core API from the UI

1. Add the plugin on both sides (`cargo add tauri-plugin-<name> --manifest-path src-tauri/Cargo.toml`, `npm install @tauri-apps/plugin-<name>`) and register it in `lib.rs` (`.plugin(tauri_plugin_<name>::init())`).
2. Grant only the permissions you need in `src-tauri/capabilities/default.json` (e.g. `"dialog:allow-open"`); the generated schema in `src-tauri/gen/schemas` completes the names in editors after a build.
3. Call it from a module in `src/api/` only. Prefer a Rust command when the operation needs validation or touches files, the network or the shell.

### Change the window, icon or bundle

- Window size, title bar, minimum size: `app.windows` in `src-tauri/tauri.conf.json`. The title bar is transparent (`titleBarStyle: "Overlay"`): elements with `data-tauri-drag-region` (the top strip of `App.vue` and `AppSidebar.vue`) move the window; keep interactive controls out of them.
- Icon: edit `app-icon.svg` (1024×1024, artwork inside the 824×824 rounded square), run `npm run tauri icon app-icon.svg`, delete the Windows/iOS/Android files it also writes (`icons/android`, `icons/ios`, `Square*.png`, `StoreLogo.png`, `icon.ico`, `64x64.png`).
- Release: bump `version` in `src-tauri/Cargo.toml` and `package.json`, run `npm run check` and `npm run build`. Builds are ad-hoc signed (`signingIdentity: "-"`) and run on this Mac; to distribute, set a Developer ID in `bundle.macOS.signingIdentity` and the `APPLE_*` notarization variables (see https://v2.tauri.app/distribute/sign/macos/). An Intel + Apple Silicon build: `rustup target add x86_64-apple-darwin`, then `npm run build -- --target universal-apple-darwin`.
- The `.dmg` gets Finder's plain window layout: `scripts/tauri.ts` sets `CI=true` for builds so they never wait for permission to control Finder. `CI=false npm run build` gets the styled window (allow the Finder automation prompt).

## Testing

- `npm test` runs Vitest and `cargo test`.
- **Rust** (`#[cfg(test)] mod tests` at the bottom of each file): services and repositories on `Database::in_memory()` (fresh, migrated, per test); `error.rs` checks what the UI receives. Commands are thin and tested through their services.
- **UI** (`test/*.test.ts`, happy-dom): stores with `setActivePinia(createPinia())` and `vi.mock('@/api/<feature>.ts')`; components with `mount()` from `@vue/test-utils`, asserting rendered output and emitted events; `test/invoke.test.ts` mocks `@tauri-apps/api/core`.
- **Scripts** (`scripts/*.test.ts`, `// @vitest-environment node`): reading `.env` and the Tauri configuration patch.
- Every new feature or bug fix gets tests in the layer where the logic lives. After UI changes, also run `npm run dev` and try the flow in the window: unit tests do not render the real WebView.

## Definition of done

1. `npm run check` passes with **zero** errors and warnings (ESLint, Clippy, Prettier, rustfmt, vue-tsc, all tests){% if choices.docker == "full" %} — run it as `docker compose run --rm dev npm run check`{% endif %}.
2. `npm run build` succeeds and the built app starts (`open src-tauri/target/release/bundle/macos/*.app`).
3. New behaviour is covered by tests, and the changed flow works in `npm run dev`.
4. Rust structs and their TypeScript mirrors (`src/api/*.ts`) match; new commands are registered in `lib.rs`; new permissions are the narrowest possible.
5. Docs match the code: `.env.example`, `README.md`{% if options.spec_md %}, `SPEC.md`{% endif %} and this file are updated when commands, env keys, structure or rules change.

## Conventions

- **Rust:** edition 2024, `cargo fmt` formatting, Clippy clean with `-D warnings`, no `unsafe`. Errors through `AppError`/`AppResult`; no `unwrap()`/`expect()` outside tests and start-up invariants. Doc comments (`///`, `//!`) on modules and public items. Logging with `log::info!`/`log::error!`, never `println!`.
- **TypeScript:** strict; no `any`, no `@ts-ignore`, no non-null assertions (`!`): narrow types instead. Erasable syntax only in `scripts/` (Node type stripping): no `enum`, `namespace` or parameter properties. Imports of project files include the extension (`@/stores/notes.ts`); type-only imports use `import type` or inline `type`.
- **Naming:** Rust modules and SQL `snake_case`; commands `verb_noun` (`list_notes`); JSON/TypeScript `camelCase` (serde `rename_all = "camelCase"`); Vue components `PascalCase.vue`, pages `<Name>Page.vue`; stores `use<Name>Store`; API clients `<name>Api`.
- **UI:** Tailwind utilities with the design tokens from `src/styles/main.css` (`bg-window`, `bg-sidebar`, `bg-panel`, `text-ink`, `text-muted`, `border-line`, `bg-accent`, `text-danger`…) instead of raw colors; light and dark follow the system appearance. Keep it native: system font, 13–14 px text, default cursor, no page-level scrolling (the content area scrolls), `select-text` only where users copy text.
- **Dependencies:** prefer the platform (Rust std, Tauri APIs, Web APIs) and existing packages; add a crate or package only when it clearly pays off. Commit `package-lock.json` and `src-tauri/Cargo.lock`. `@tauri-apps/api` and the `tauri` crate must stay on the same minor version (and `@tauri-apps/cli` with them).
- **npm install scripts** of dependencies run only when `allowScripts` in `package.json` allows them (npm 11+). Review new ones with `npm install-scripts ls`; approve a package only when it really needs its script. `fsevents` is denied: it ships a prebuilt binary.
- Let the formatters format (`npm run format`); do not disable lint rules to get green.
- Write code, comments, UI texts and docs in English.
